<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ranthambore Fort</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ranastambhapura, Ranthambhor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ranthambore Fort is a hill fortress in Sawai Madhopur district of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> as Ranastambhapura. It was the seat of a Chauhan dynasty from the 1190s until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> captured it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammira-deva">Hammira Deva</a> in 1301, and later passed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> sultans, Mewar and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, who took it in 1569. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-forts-of-rajasthan">Hill Forts of Rajasthan</a> World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranthambore-fort.jpg" alt="Ranthambore Fort" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manojmeena · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ranthambhore_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ranthambore Fort stands within Ranthambore National Park near Sawai Madhopur in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. The park was the hunting ground of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a>. The fortress commands a strategic position 700 feet above the surrounding plain, and was considered impregnable owing to its site and design, which is one reason why so many rulers wanted it. It gives a panoramic view of the park and is now a popular tourist attraction. In 2013 the World Heritage Committee, meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>, declared it, with five other forts of Rajasthan, a World Heritage Site under the group <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-forts-of-rajasthan">Hill Forts of Rajasthan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its earlier name was Ranastambhapura, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for City of the Battle Post. It was associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> under Prithviraja I of the Chauhan dynasty in the 12th century, when Siddhasenasuri listed it among the holy Jain tirthas. After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraja III</a> in 1192 the fort came under the Ghurid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a>. The Delhi Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a> captured it in 1226, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a> retook it after his death in 1236. Forces of Sultan Nasiruddin Mahmud, led by the future Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balban">Balban</a>, besieged it unsuccessfully in 1248 and 1253 and took it from Jaitrasingh Chauhan in 1259. Shakti Dev succeeded Jaitrasingh in 1283, recaptured the fort and enlarged the kingdom.</p><p>Sultan Jalal ud din Firuz Khalji besieged it without success in 1290-91. In 1299 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammira-deva">Hammiradeva</a> sheltered Muhammad Shah, a rebel general of Sultan Ala ud din Khalji, and refused to hand him over, and the Sultan besieged and took the fort in 1301.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later the fortress was held by various kings of Mewar, directly under Rana Hamir Singh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a>. In the reign of Rana Udai Singh I it passed to the Hada Rajputs of Bundi, and Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-gujarat">Bahadur Shah of Gujarat</a> held it briefly from 1532 to 1535. The Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> took it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hada-chauhans">Hadas</a> in the Siege of Ranthambore. In the 17th century it passed to the Kachwaha Maharajas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> and remained part of Jaipur state until independence; Jaipur acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1949 and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> in 1950.</p><p>Within the fort are three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> as Trinetra Ganesha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and Ramlalaji, built in the 12th and 13th centuries from red Karauli stone, and a Jain temple of Sumatinath and Sambhavanath. The Ganesha temple draws thousands of visitors every Wednesday.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ranthambore Fort” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bundi's Raos often fought Mewar and later served the Mughals: Rao Surjan surrendered <b>Ranthambore</b> to Akbar in 1569, and Rao Chhatrasal was killed at Samugarh in 1658 fighting for Dara Shikoh.</blockquote><a href="/state/bundi-state">Bundi State</a></li><li><blockquote>After Prithviraj Chauhan (Prithviraja III) of the Chahamanas of Shakambhari fell to Muhammad of Ghor in 1192, his son Govindaraja was installed as a Ghurid vassal and moved to the fortress of <b>Ranthambore</b> (Ranastambhapura) in eastern Rajasthan.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-ranastambhapura">Chahamanas of Ranastambhapura</a></li><li><blockquote>A relative ruled Ajmer under Ghurid protection until 1194, and the branch at <b>Ranthambore</b> continued.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-shakambhari">Chahamanas of Shakambhari</a></li></ul></section>
